T1  - Letter to the editor: Effects of virtual reality cognitive training on executive function and prospective memory in Parkinson's disease and healthy aging
AN  - 3292727523; 2027-03077-001
AB  - Comments on the article by Eleonora Fiorenzato et al. (see record 2026-20663-001). The study’s exploration of immersive virtual reality (iVR) combined with telemedicine to target executive functions (EF) for cognitive improvement in Parkinson’s disease with mild cognitive impairment (PD-MCI) and healthy older adults (HC) addresses a critical gap in nonpharmacological interventions. Its finding of iVR EF training enhancing time-based/verbal response prospective memory (PM) and inhibition in PD-MCI, plus planning in HC, aligns with emerging evidence for iVR’s ecological validity in cognitive rehabilitation. The present author notes some key limitations that hinder its full clinical translation. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved)
