TY  - JOUR
T1  - Reply: Reflections on the cognitive training of immersive virtual reality in executive function and prospective memory for PD-MCI patients and healthy older adults
AN  - 3257501820; 2026-57655-001
AB  - Reply by the current authors to the comments made by S. Liu et al. (see record 2026-57654-001) on the original article (see record 2026-20663-001). Fiorenzato et al fully acknowledge that a major limitation of their study was the small sample size, which included participants with PD with mild cognitive impairment (PD-MCI) and healthy older adults. According to the study design, these were randomly allocated into either the experimental or active-placebo control group, resulting in 15 participants per group. In turn, this has possibly limited statistical power and increased the risk of false-negative results. Therefore, Fiorenzato et al recognize the importance of replicating the current study in a larger sample to confirm their findings. Concerning the selective improvement in the time-based prospective memory (PM) task, the findings seem to support the notion that time-based and event-based PM leverage on different underlying cognitive processes: time-based tasks rely more on executive functions whereas event-based tasks rely more on retrospective memory. Namely, event-based PM tasks requires an effective cue-intensions association—that is, the cue detection may not lead to action, if the intention retrieval fails due to memory dysfunctions. Thus, based on previous evidence, Fiorenzato et al can hypothesize that CT programs targeting both executive and memory functions may be more effective in enhancing event-based PM in individuals with PDMCI. Future research will need to explore whether longer interventions, as well as multimodal approaches integrating CT with physical activity, might yield broader benefits— especially in untrained domains such as mood, quality of life, and functional independence, which are closely tied to motor symptoms in PD. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
