TY  - JOUR
T1  - Reflections on the cognitive training of immersive virtual reality in executive function and prospective memory for PD-MCI patients and healthy older adults
AN  - 3257501129; 2026-57654-001
AB  - Comments on an article by E. Fiorenzato et al. (see record 2026-20663-001). The article by Fiorenzato et al. provides corresponding evidence that cognitive training in immersive virtual reality (VR) is effective in improving executive performance and prospective memory in PD-MCI patients and healthy older adults. The findings emphasize the innovative, accessible, and scalable nature of immersive virtual reality as a tool for cognitive rehabilitation, and we are deeply grateful to the authors for conducting such a relevant and comprehensive study that provides valuable insights for clinical practice and future research. The present authors raise some points for consideraton regarding sample size, baseline cognitive differences between groups and covenience vs cost-effectiveness of and feasibility of VR + telemedicine. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
