TY  - JOUR
T1  - Cognitive functioning and depressive symptoms among older adults in China: The role of alcohol use and gender
AN  - 3271983102; 2026-66301-003
AB  - Objective: This study aimed to examine the role alcohol use and gender played in the association between cognitive function and depressive symptoms among older adults. Methods: Using the China Health and Retirement Longitudinal Study baseline survey, cognitive function was measured by figure drawing, telephone interview of cognitive status-10, and word recall tests. Depression was measured by Center for Epidemiologic Studies Depression Scale short form. Multiple regression models were conducted separately to examine their associations for males and females among alcohol use groups while controlling for covariates. Results: Significant negative associations were found between domains of cognitive functioning and depressive symptoms across different alcohol user groups (Bs range: −6.21 to −0.14, 
ps all < .05). Moreover, the effects were stronger in females compared with males. Conclusions: Different cognitive components should be prioritized based on older adults’ alcohol use when helping them reduce depressive symptoms. Older women warrant additional attention due to their cognitive decline being more pronounced deterioration in depressive symptoms compared with older men. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract) Impact statementWhat does this research add to existing knowledge in gerontology? This research sheds light on the complex interplay between cognitive decline, alcohol use, gender, and depressive symptoms. The findings suggest that addressing cognitive function, particularly in the context of alcohol use, could be a promising approach for reducing depressive symptoms in older adults. Additionally, the study highlights the importance of considering gender differences in cognitive decline and its impact on mental health. What are the implications of this new knowledge for nursing care with older people? Nurses can use these findings to improve their assessments and interventions with older adults, particularly in relation to identifying and addressing cognitive impairments and alcohol use. Additionally, nurses can use this information to tailor care to the specific needs of older women who may be at greater risk for experiencing more severe depressive symptoms. How could the findings be used to influence policy or practice or research or education? This study highlights the need for further investigation into the effectiveness of cognitive training interventions in reducing depressive symptoms in older adults with alcohol use. Researchers could build on this study by conducting randomized controlled trials to test the effectiveness of tailored cognitive training interventions. These findings could then be integrated into nursing education programs to ensure that future nurses are equipped with the knowledge and skills necessary to provide effective care for older adults with alcohol use and depressive symptoms. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
