TY  - JOUR
T1  - Cognitive, symptomatic, and pharmacological factors contributing to objective social functioning improvement in schizophrenia rehabilitation: A prospective study
AN  - 3242186506; 2026-05049-012
AB  - This letter presents a study which aims to examine cognitive, symptomatic, and pharmacological factors contributing to objective social functioning improvement in schizophrenia rehabilitation. A total of 270 patients with DSM-IV-TR diagnosed schizophrenia from 18 psychiatric hospitals across China were enrolled, with 235 patients completing a 24-week rehabilitation program. Participants were adults aged 18–65 years. Those with severe medical conditions, cognitive impairment, or active substance use disorders were exlcuded. The program consisted of three 8-week phases: medication management, symptom management, and social skills training. Led by specially trained nurses and psychiatrists, participants attended 45-min sessions twice a week, involving educational components, role-playing, and hands-on activities. Objective functional outcomes were assessed using the Personal and Social Performance Scale (PSP), which comprises four dimensions (PSP1 to PSP4). Clinical symptoms were evaluated using the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS), Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9), and Generalized Anxiety Disorder Scale (GAD-7). Cognitive functions were measured using the Ascertain Dementia 8 (AD8), while extrapyramidal side effects were assessed using the Rating Scale for Extrapyramidal Side Effects (RSESE). PSP improvement negatively correlated with baseline AD8 scores and positively correlated with improvements in negative symptoms, hostility/suspiciousness, and AD8. Similar correlations were found for subdomain improvements (Fig. S2). Regression analysis identified AD8 improvement (B = 0.07, P < 0.001) and baseline PSP2 (B =-0.08, P < 0.001) as significant predictors of total PSP improvement (R 2 = 0.58). Multivariate analysis, incorporating all evaluation items and baseline characteristics, revealed significant associations for changes in total PSP score, AD8 score, and baseline PSP2 score. Specific PSP domains were significantly predicted by baseline anxiety/depression, negative symptoms, and AD8 improvement. The results suggest that cognitive function improvement and baseline personal and social relationships strongly predict overall functioning improvement, under scoring the importance of cognitive training in rehabilitation programs for schizophrenia. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
