TY  - JOUR
T1  - Cognitive training as a shield against stress-induced working memory deficits
AN  - 3117623946; 2025-34323-005
AB  - Comments on an article by K. Loock & L. Schwabe (see record 2025-20493-001). Loock and Schwabe confirmed that training was effective, as evidenced by an increase in the average number of points earned from the first to the last training days in both groups. Of note, the intervention was more challenging, resulting in lower points earned and lower difficulty levels in this group than in the sham group. Loock and Schwabe then tested whether the cognitive training could prevent stress-induced working memory impairments. Approximately 25 minutes after stressor onset, when salivary cortisol levels were expected to peak, participants began cognitive testing, consisting of an n-back task to assess working memory performance under stress. The n-back task involved identifying whether a number shown on a screen matched one presented several trials earlier, with 2 difficulty levels implemented in 4 blocks of 30 trials each. Loock and Schwabe advance our understanding of cognitive resilience and open new avenues for research and intervention. The potential to protect against stress-induced and cognitive decline through a simple, noninvasive training program could have important implications for mental health and, eventually, aging. Future research should focus on refining and personalizing cognitive training to ensure that its benefits are broadly accessible, particularly for those most at risk. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
