TY  - JOUR
T1  - Dementia in Taiwan
AN  - 3230149485; 2024-73150-001
AB  - Comments on an article 
Trends in dementia incidence and mortality, and dynamic changes in comorbidity and healthcare utilization from 2004 to 2017: A Taiwan national cohort study by S. T. Huang et al. (2024). Huang et al. report results from a study examining the national dementia incidence rate and accompanying health- and care-related outcomes in Taiwan. Huang and colleagues used data spanning 2004 to 2017 collected from the Taiwan National Health Insurance, which covers more than 99 % of people in Taiwan, to assess annual rates of dementia. Despite only a small increase in age-standardized rates of incident dementia, the absolute number of people diagnosed with dementia increased from 30,6060 in 2004 to 50,651 in 2017, likely due to overall population aging. While Alzheimer’s disease accounted for 75 % of dementia diagnoses in the Huang et al. study, 16 % were vascular dementia cases, which notably showed a steep drop in annual incidence starting in 2011. Huang et al. also examined the medications prescribed for people with dementia. A class of medications that is recommended by Taiwanese clinical care guidelines as the first choice for treatment following a dementia diagnosis are cholinesterase inhibitors, which may be used to improve cognitive function, particularly in mild-moderate Alzheimer’s disease and when prescribed early. However, the results reported by Huang and colleagues suggest the use of anti-dementia medications was limited, with only 5 % of patients receiving a prescription in the first three months post-diagnosis and only 9 % having any anti-dementia prescriptions in five years. One limitation of the Huang et al. study concerns what remains uncaptured: while the data suggest that medications are being prescribed to manage behavioral symptoms of dementia, despite clinical guidelines cautioning against this, what’s missing is the non-pharmacological interventions that are, or are not, being used. Physical exercise, occupational therapy, cognitive training, and sensory therapy are only some of the interventions that have some evidence of improving BPSD, cognitive function, or quality of life in people with dementia and are recommended by the Taiwanese clinical care guidelines. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
