TY  - JOUR
T1  - The potential compensatory effect of transcranial electrical stimulation on the adverse impact of white matter damage in the aging brain
AN  - 3074139113; 2024-98628-028
AB  - The article presents a study indicating a potential prognostic value of individual white matter damage on training-induced cognitive plasticity in the aging brain. Whether or not white matter damage is related to brain stimulation-induced cognitive plasticity has not yet been reported. Authors aimed to explore the potential role of age-related white matter damage for tES-induced performance gains in older adults. Authors used data from two of their recent randomized controlled trials that investigated the effects of a combined tES-plus-training intervention in older adults on the continuum from healthy aging to mild cognitive impairment. This is the first exploratory analysis of a link between WMH volume and tDCS-induced performance gains. Among various baseline characteristics with impact on interventional outcomes in the aging brain, WMH has only recently gained attention, reflecting that age related brain tissue alterations such as demyelination, axonal and glial loss may mediate the effects of plasticity-inducing interventions. Authors findings extend these initial reports by observing a potential compensatory effect of tES on the adverse impact of WMH volumes. Thus, white matter damage might be a significant biomarker, predicting the individual benefit of tDCS-accompanied cognitive training intervention. In addition to a predictive value of baseline WMH of tDCS responsiveness, which was the focus of the current analysis, tDCS may have an impact on white matter damage itself (e.g., by reducing neuroinflammatory responses (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
