TY  - JOUR
T1  - Commentary on “Multimodal exercise and cognitive training program improves cognitive function in amnestic mild cognitive impairment”
AN  - 3045503874; 2024-66462-009
AB  - Comments on an article by K. Fairchild et al (see record 2024-45965-001). Because of the progressively larger aging population, Alzheimer disease (AD) poses a major and increasing public health crisis in the United States and globally. Despite its prevalence and costs, AD is the only disease among the top 10 causes of death in the United States with no cure. Mild cognitive impairment (MCI), a prodromal state between normal cognitive aging and early dementia syndromes, including AD, presents a crucial window of opportunity for preventive intervention strategies and early treatments. Fairchild et al reported findings from a twogroup randomized clinical trial comparing two combined physical exercise and cognitive training interventions in older adults with amnestic mild cognitive impairment (aMCI), one involving combined aerobic and resistance exercise and cognitive training (CARE +CT), and the other involving combined stretching exercise and cognitive training (SE+CT). One of the strengths of this program is that it includes both a “pre-training” phase that improves participants’ readiness for the program as well as a mnemonic training phase involving training in specific, well established techniques such as method-of-loci and name-face mnemonics. The Fairchild et al study is consistent t with recent trends toward the use of multimodal interventions with cognitively impaired individuals and the increased focus on the predictors or mechanisms underlying treatment effects. It is noted that an important conclusion of the Fairchild et al study is that the field must continue to explore multimodal interventions to reduce the risk fo neurodegenerative disorders using fully powered randomized controlled designs with diverse samples of older adults with a aMCI. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
