TY  - JOUR
T1  - Qigong reduces depressive symptoms of Taiwanese elderly with chronic physical illness: A randomized controlled trial
AN  - 2489751023; 2020-06039-005
AB  - Presents a study which aims to examine the effects of Qigong on the reduction of depressive symptoms of Taiwanese elderly with chronic physical illness. Thirty older adults of age 60 years or older with depressive symptoms and chronic physical illness were recruited from the Chong-You Nursing Home and Yu-You Nursing Home of Chong-En Group, Taiwan. They were randomly assigned to either intervention group or control group by research staff blind to research hypotheses using a random number generator. Participants in intervention group received individual Eight-Section Brocades qigong training twice per week for 12 weeks. Participants in the control group received 12 weeks of cognitive training involving activities such as paired card recognition. The cognitive training was conducted in small groups, and included activities involving upper arm mobilization. The effects of qigong on depressive symptoms and other secondary outcomes were tested using repeated measures analysis of variance. The present randomized controlled trial (RCT) demonstrated antidepressive effects of qigong for older adults with chronic physical illness and depressive symptoms in Taiwan, replicating previous research. In addition, these findings extend that previous research by showing that qigong-related improvements also occur in a number of additional secondary outcome domains. One important limitation of this study is the possibility that some or all of the between-group differences observed could have been due to differences in how the treatments were provided. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
