TY  - JOUR
T1  - Cognitive training for older adults: What works?
AN  - 2214502880; 2018-17657-001
AB  - Comments on an article by E. Kallio et al. (see record 2018-17657-006). Kallio and colleagues report that 12 weeks of twice-weekly training in executive function skills did not show a significant benefit in reducing cognitive impairment. The study was well designed and well executed. How does this negative study fit into the larger body of literature on cognitive training for older adults? The simplest answer is that there have been many clinical trials of cognitive training for older adults, including studies in healthy older adults, those with mild cognitive impairment, and those with dementia. Many of the positive studies used higher-intensity cognitive training than the Kallio study, so this new study, albeit negative, does not disprove the benefits of cognitive training for older adults but gives us important information about the intensity, length, and type of treatments that might need to be explored in future work. In particular, its findings are in line with those suggesting that cognitive training might not work well for older adults who already have dementia. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
