TY  - JOUR
T1  - Review ofImproving memory through creativity: A professional’s guide to culturally sensitive cognitive training with older adults
AN  - 1779462625; 2016-10999-007
AB  - Reviews the book, 
Improving Memory through Creativity: A Professional's Guide to Culturally Sensitive Cognitive Training with Older Adults by Amanda Alders Pike (2014). The author states the book is aimed at, ‘many different professionals. . .to benefit diverse older adults’ and although she offers quite structured Art Therapy sessions, she does clarify that the work can only be called ‘therapy’ by trained professionals. This does cause issues throughout the book as she swings from simplistic descriptions of creativity, Art Therapy and the structure of sessions, whilst also offering in-depth analysis of well over 250 references of cognitive and Art Therapy research and neuroscience. It is clear from the start that the author’s work is with a client group with very mild-to-moderate cognitive impairment, and this slightly relieves my anxiety with the notion of ‘improving memory’. Cognitive-based therapy feels very popular at the moment and I became less sceptical of the benefits of it working within Arts Therapies contexts in reading the book, but it is a very different way of working and I feel I would need to know a lot more before embarking on this work. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
