TY  - JOUR
T1  - The effect of cognitive and acupoint massage on cognitive functions of the elderly
AN  - 904016946; 2011-20320-035
AB  - Objective: To compare the effectiveness of the cognitive training and acupoint massage on cognitive functions of the elderly. Methods: 144 old people from a community in Changsha City were randomly assigned to the control group, cognitive training group, and acupoint massage group. All the training lasted six months. At baseline and six months follow-up, all participants' data were measured by the Mini-mental state examination (MMSE) and the picture recall, logical memory, word association, and digit span subscales of Wechsler Memory Scale China Revised edition (WMS-C). Results: Both cognitive training and self-massage significantly improved the MMSE score, as well as the picture recall, logical memory, and word association scores of the elderly (
P < 0.01); but there was no significant difference between the cognitive training and acupoint massage group (
P > 0.05). Conclusion: Cognitive training and acupoint massage can both significantly improve cognitive functions of the elderly. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2024 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract)
