TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effects of cognitive training interventions with older adults: A randomized controlled trial
AN  - 619813292; 2002-06698-001
AB  - Cognitive function in older adults is related to independent living and need for care. This study evaluated whether 3 cognitive training interventions improve mental abilities and daily functioning in older, independent-living adults. 2,832 persons aged 65-94 yrs were randomly assigned to 1 of 4 groups: 10-session group training for verbal episodic memory, reasoning, speed of processing (visual search and identification); or a no-contact control group. For the 3 treatment groups, 4-session booster training was offered to a 60% random sample 11 mo later. Each intervention improved the targeted cognitive ability compared with baseline, durable to 2 yrs. 87% of speed-, 74% of reasoning-, and 26% of memory-trained participants demonstrated reliable cognitive improvement immediately after the intervention period. Booster training enhanced training gains in speed and reasoning interventions, which were maintained at 2-yr follow-up. No training effects on everyday functioning were detected at 2 yrs. Results support the effectiveness and durability of the cognitive training interventions in improving targeted cognitive abilities. Because of minimal functional decline across all groups, longer followup is likely required to observe training effects on everyday function. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
